@prefix : <#> .
@prefix schema: <http://schema.org/> .
@prefix xsd: <http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#> .
@prefix rdfs: <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#> .

<> a schema:CreativeWork ;
    schema:name "howto/secret-resolution-in-generated-skills.ttl"@en ;
    schema:description "A skill this agent authors or packages MUST NOT carry a secret literal in its source, and MUST resolve secrets through the same four-source chain used for the principal PKCS#12 passphrase: explicit argument, environment variable, OS credential store, hidden interactive prompt. Generalises p12-passphrase-retrieval.ttl from the one standing credential to any secret in any generated artifact."@en ;
    schema:dateCreated "2026-08-19T00:00:00Z"^^xsd:dateTime ;
    schema:dateModified "2026-08-19T00:00:00Z"^^xsd:dateTime ;
    schema:author <https://linkedin.com/in/kidehen#this> ;
    schema:about :howto .

:howto a schema:HowTo ;
    schema:name "Resolve secrets in generated skills — credential store first, prompt last, never a source literal"@en ;
    schema:description "Established 2026-08-19 while generating the x402-buyer skill. The user asked whether a throwaway EVM private key shipped in the source script could be moved to the Keychain and prompted for when required. The answer generalises: no generated skill should ever hold a secret literal, regardless of how valueless that particular secret is."@en ;
    schema:step :step-noSecretLiterals, :step-fourSourceChain, :step-storeCreation,
        :step-reportKeySource, :step-throwawayIsNotAnException, :step-migrationProcedure,
        :step-crossPlatformDocumentedNotAssumed ;
    rdfs:seeAlso <../preferences.ttl#step-secretResolutionInGeneratedSkills>,
        <p12-passphrase-retrieval.ttl>,
        <credential-location-session-start.ttl>,
        <../../x402-buyer/SKILL.md> .

# ── Step 1: no secret literals in generated source ───────────────────────────

:step-noSecretLiterals a schema:HowToStep ;
    schema:position "1"^^xsd:integer ;
    schema:name "A skill, script, or example this agent authors MUST NOT contain a secret literal — key, passphrase, token, or API key"@en ;
    schema:text """A secret in a source file is a secret in every clone, diff, branch, backup, and packaged ZIP of that repo. Removing it later does not remove it from history.

This applies to the packaging step specifically: when building a skill AROUND a user-supplied script, inheriting the script's embedded secret is a decision, not a neutral copy. Flag it at delivery and offer the credential-store migration in the same breath.

DETECTION before packaging any skill — run over the whole skill directory and again over the built ZIP:
  grep -rEn '0x[0-9a-fA-F]{64}' .                    # EVM private keys
  grep -rEn '(sk_live|sk_test|ghp_|xox[baprs]-)[A-Za-z0-9]+' .   # common token shapes
Public identifiers (wallet ADDRESSES, WebIDs, account names) are not secrets and should stay — they are what let the user verify which wallet is about to spend."""@en .

# ── Step 2: the four-source chain ────────────────────────────────────────────

:step-fourSourceChain a schema:HowToStep ;
    schema:position "2"^^xsd:integer ;
    schema:name "Resolve in strict order: explicit argument, environment variable, OS credential store, hidden prompt"@en ;
    schema:text """SOURCE 1 — explicit CLI argument (--key and equivalents). Support it, but document it as DISCOURAGED: it lands in shell history and is visible in `ps` for the process lifetime. Never hand the user a command with a secret inline.

SOURCE 2 — environment variable. The right answer for CI and scripted runs.

SOURCE 3 — OS credential store. THE NORMAL PATH. Prefer the Python `keyring` package (one API, native backend per OS: macOS Keychain, Secret Service on Linux, Credential Manager on Windows), falling back to `/usr/bin/security` on macOS so the skill works without the extra dependency. A keyring-created and a security-created item are the same item on macOS.

SOURCE 4 — hidden interactive prompt via getpass, never echoed. After a successful prompt, OFFER to save to the credential store so the prompt happens once per secret rather than once per run.

Falling through 1 to 4 is silent and normal — an absent credential-store item is an expected condition, not an error to report.

NON-TTY GUARD: when every source misses and stdin is not a terminal, exit with a message naming the service and account that were tried plus the exact command to store the value. Do not hang waiting on a prompt nobody can answer.

VALIDATE the resolved value's shape (e.g. 0x + 64 hex) before use, so a store holding an address instead of a key fails with a clear message rather than deep inside a signing library."""@en .

# ── Step 3: creating the store item ──────────────────────────────────────────

:step-storeCreation a schema:HowToStep ;
    schema:position "3"^^xsd:integer ;
    schema:name "The USER creates the item via the interactive bare -w form; -T /usr/bin/security is mandatory"@en ;
    schema:text """CANONICAL CREATION — the bare -w takes the value from a hidden prompt, so the secret never passes through the agent, a command line, or shell history:
  security add-generic-password -s SERVICE -a ACCOUNT -T /usr/bin/security -w

-T /usr/bin/security is REQUIRED, not optional. It puts the security binary on the item's ACL so later reads return the value directly. Without it macOS raises a "security wants to access..." GUI dialog and a non-interactive read HANGS. Per p12-passphrase-retrieval.ttl :step-keychainPrimary.

READ GUARD, unchanged from the p12 convention:
  perl -e 'alarm 8; exec @ARGV' security find-generic-password -s SERVICE -a ACCOUNT -w
`timeout` does not exist on stock macOS; perl's alarm is the portable guard. A skill's own resolver must apply this guard so a mis-ACL'd item degrades to a prompt instead of hanging.

NAMING — service is the skill's namespace (x402-buyer-evm-key); account is a LABEL for which instance of the secret, exposed as a CLI flag (--key-account) with a "default" default. This lets several wallets/tenants coexist under one service without touching each other, and satisfies the namespacing requirement of :step-neverProbeProductionCredentialNames.

BEFORE WRITING any credential-store item, check the service/account pair is free with a plain read, and use a plain add rather than -U. -U is a write-with-overwrite; against an unexpected existing item it silently destroys the user's value. See p12-passphrase-retrieval.ttl :step-neverProbeProductionCredentialNames for the incident that established this."""@en .

# ── Step 4: report which source resolved ─────────────────────────────────────

:step-reportKeySource a schema:HowToStep ;
    schema:position "4"^^xsd:integer ;
    schema:name "Print and report WHICH source resolved the secret — never the value"@en ;
    schema:text """A resolver that silently picks a source hides which identity is acting. For anything that spends money or signs, print a "Key source: ..." line and surface it in the agent's report alongside the derived public identifier (wallet address, WebID, key fingerprint) so the user can confirm the right principal before the irreversible step.

Record in RDF memory only which source resolved and whether the operation succeeded. Never copy a secret into preferences.ttl, a session file, a howto, a commit message, an artifact, or the chat transcript — per p12-passphrase-retrieval.ttl :step-redact."""@en .

# ── Step 5: a throwaway secret is not an exception ───────────────────────────

:step-throwawayIsNotAnException a schema:HowToStep ;
    schema:position "5"^^xsd:integer ;
    schema:name "A throwaway or testnet credential does not license a source literal"@en ;
    schema:text """The x402_get.py docstring argued its committed key was safe because it was Base Sepolia testnet-only, held no value, and was committed deliberately so anyone could rerun the smoke test. Every one of those claims was true, and the conclusion still did not follow.

WHY IT STILL COMES OUT:
  1. The pattern is the payload. A reader copying the working example copies the shape — key in the docstring — and the next one has value.
  2. Valuelessness is a property of TODAY. Nothing stops a testnet address being reused or funded later.
  3. Secret scanners fire on it regardless, and the noise trains people to dismiss real alerts.
  4. The convenience it buys is one-time setup, which the credential-store path already provides at the cost of a single prompt.

WHAT TO KEEP: the public address, so the test identity stays documented and verifiable, plus an explicit note that the private key now lives in the credential store. The user loses nothing and the example teaches the right pattern."""@en .

# ── Step 6: the migration procedure ──────────────────────────────────────────

:step-migrationProcedure a schema:HowToStep ;
    schema:position "6"^^xsd:integer ;
    schema:name "Migration order: verify free, add, verify readback, THEN strip from source — never strip first"@en ;
    schema:text """Executed 2026-08-19 for x402-buyer. Order matters: stripping the literal before proving the store works destroys the only copy.

  1. VERIFY FREE — plain read of the service/account pair; confirm "could not be found". Never -U over an item of unknown provenance.
  2. ADD — extract the value from the existing file programmatically rather than typing it into the command, so the literal never appears in the agent's visible command text:
       KEYVAL=$(grep -oE '0x[0-9a-fA-F]{64}' script.py | head -1)
       security add-generic-password -s SERVICE -a ACCOUNT -D "..." -j "..." -T /usr/bin/security -w "$KEYVAL"
       unset KEYVAL
     (For a REAL secret, do not do this at all — have the user run the interactive bare -w form per :step-storeCreation. The programmatic path is acceptable only when the value is already sitting in plaintext in a file the agent legitimately read, which is exactly the case being remediated.)
  3. VERIFY READBACK — non-interactive read under the alarm guard, compared against the source value; a mismatch means STOP and do not strip.
  4. STRIP — remove the literal from source, replacing it with a pointer to the store coordinates. Keep the public address.
  5. RE-SWEEP — grep the skill directory AND the rebuilt ZIP for the secret shape.
  6. TEST the resolver's full precedence chain against the real store item, including the absent-item fall-through and the non-tty exit.

RESIDUAL COPIES: the original source archive and any prior git history still contain the secret. Say so plainly at delivery — a migration that leaves the user believing the secret is gone everywhere is worse than one they know is partial."""@en .

# ── Step 7: document the cross-platform gap honestly, don't assume it away ───

:step-crossPlatformDocumentedNotAssumed a schema:HowToStep ;
    schema:position "7"^^xsd:integer ;
    schema:name "The keyring-first resolver code IS cross-platform by construction, but the DOCS must say so explicitly per-OS and must not claim testing that did not happen"@en ;
    schema:text """Follow-up correction, 2026-08-19, same x402-buyer session, after a /model switch to Sonnet 5. The resolver already tried the `keyring` package before any macOS-specific fallback, so Linux/Windows support existed in the code from the first pass. But the shipped docs called `pip install keyring` uniformly \"optional\", which is only true on macOS -- on Linux/Windows there is no fallback binary, so without it the resolver silently skips straight to a prompt on every run. A user reading \"optional\" would reasonably not install it and get a materially worse experience with no explanation why.

FIX: state the keyring requirement PER-OS, not as one blanket word, in every place it's mentioned (script docstring, SKILL.md Prerequisites, references/setup.md). Added a dedicated Cross-Platform Notes section (SKILL.md) with a per-OS backend table.

SEPARATE, DO-NOT-SKIP CAVEAT: headless Linux (a server or container with no Secret Service daemon) is a real failure mode inherited from p12-passphrase-retrieval.ttl :step-crossPlatformKeyring -- `keyring` there may raise OR silently route to a non-secure/null backend rather than a real encrypted store. Give the reader the exact check (`python3 -c \"import keyring; print(keyring.get_keyring())\"`) and the escape hatch ($EVM_PRIVATE_KEY from their own secret manager) rather than implying the credential store always works once the package is installed.

HONESTY REQUIREMENT: this agent authored and can only directly test the macOS path (a real Keychain item, six precedence-chain assertions, verified read/write). The Linux/Windows paths follow from `keyring`'s own documented routing but were NOT independently exercised here. Said so explicitly in both SKILL.md and setup.md rather than letting silence imply equal verification across all three platforms. General principle: a claim of cross-platform support built on a well-known library's documented behavior is not the same claim as cross-platform support that was actually run and observed -- state which one is being made."""@en ;
    rdfs:seeAlso <../../x402-buyer/SKILL.md#cross-platform-notes>,
        <p12-passphrase-retrieval.ttl>,
        <model-identity-resolution.ttl> .
